    <body>
      <div type="clause" n="2">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:IVAtr">IV Atr</ref> 2</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="2" corresp="#wit-q"><pb n="232" ed="#liebermann"/>Ad Billingesgate<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="5" target="#IVAtr_2.lnote.oe.5"/> si aduenisset una nauicula, I obolus<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="6" target="#IVAtr_2.lnote.oe.6"/> tolonei<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="7" target="#IVAtr_2.lnote.oe.7"/> dabatur, si maior et haberet<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="8" target="#IVAtr_2.lnote.oe.8"/> siglas<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="9" target="#IVAtr_2.lnote.oe.9"/>, I<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="10" target="#IVAtr_2.lnote.oe.10"/> d.</ab>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="la" source="#robertson" n="2" corresp="#wit-q"><pb n="70" ed="#robertson"/>Ad Billingesgate si advenisset una navicula—I obolus tolonei<ptr type="editorNote" n="12" target="#IVAtr_2.robnote.fn.12"/> dabatur; si maior et haberet<ptr type="editorNote" n="13" target="#IVAtr_2.robnote.fn.13"/> siglas<ptr type="editorNote" n="14" target="#IVAtr_2.robnote.fn.14"/>— unum den.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="de" source="#liebermann"><pb n="233" ed="#liebermann"/>Wenn an Billingsgate<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#IVAtr_2.lnote.de.1"/> ein kleines Schiff gelandet war, wurde ½ Pfennig<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#IVAtr_2.lnote.de.2"/> Zoll gegeben, wenn ein grösseres und [welches] Segel<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="3" target="#IVAtr_2.lnote.de.3"/> hat: 1 Pfennig.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude" ana="#fromGerman">If a small ship had landed at Billingsgate, ½ penny toll was given; if a larger one, and [one which] has sails: 1 penny.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#robertson" corresp="#wit-q"><pb n="71" ed="#robertson"/>If a small ship came to Billingsgate<ptr type="editorNote" n="1" target="#IVAtr_2.robnote.n.1"/>, 1 half-penny was paid as toll; if a larger ship with sails, 1 penny was paid.</ab>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IVAtr_2.lnote.oe.5" n="5" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">232</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Bili. <hi rend="italic">TMHk;</hi> Bilynggesg. <hi rend="italic">Br</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Bili. TMHk; Bilynggesg. Br</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IVAtr_2.lnote.oe.6" n="6" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">232</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">-lum <hi rend="italic">R;</hi> obulus <hi rend="italic">T</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">-lum R; obulus T</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IVAtr_2.lnote.oe.7" n="7" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">232</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">thel. <hi rend="italic">T</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">thel. T</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IVAtr_2.lnote.oe.8" n="8" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">232</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">habet <hi rend="italic">TMHkBr</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">habet TMHkBr</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IVAtr_2.lnote.oe.9" n="9" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">232</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">glas, <hi rend="italic">darüber</hi> si <hi rend="italic">M;</hi> gulas <hi rend="italic">RT</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">glas, with si above it M; gulas RT</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IVAtr_2.lnote.oe.10" n="10" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">232</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">unum denarium <hi rend="italic">RT</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">unum denarium RT</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IVAtr_2.lnote.de.1" n="1" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">163</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Unterhalb London Bridge, von dem die Themse Hinauffahrenden zuerst erreicht.—Zur Ableitung des Namens <hi rend="italic">vgl</hi>. die Männernamen <hi rend="italic">Bil, Billa, Billing</hi> (Searle <hi rend="italic">Onomasticon</hi>) und die Orte <hi rend="italic">Billingbroc, -den, -gaburg, -hoch</hi> Kemble <hi rend="italic">Cod. dipl</hi>. VI 259; Sim. Dunelm. a. 798.—Billingsgate war Zollstätte unter Richard I.; Madox <hi rend="italic">Hist. Exchequer</hi> 531. <hi rend="italic">Vgl. Gl</hi> Zollabgabe 1—2d</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Below London Bridge, the first reached by those sailing up the Thames.—On the derivation of the name <hi rend="italic">cf</hi>. the men's names <hi rend="italic">Bil, Billa, Billing</hi> (Searle <hi rend="italic">Onomasticon</hi>) and the places <hi rend="italic">Billingbroc, -den, -gaburg, -hoch</hi> Kemble <hi rend="italic">Cod. dipl</hi>. VI 259; Sim. Dunelm. a. 798.—Billingsgate was a toll station under Richard I; Madox <hi rend="italic">Hist. Exchequer</hi> 531. <hi rend="italic">Cf. Gl</hi> Zollabgabe 1—2d</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IVAtr_2.lnote.de.2" n="2" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">163</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">healf penig</hi> las wohl Orig., laut <hi rend="italic">Wb obolata</hi>. <hi rend="italic">Vgl. Gl</hi> Halbpfennig; zum Marktzoll Maitland <hi rend="italic">Domesday</hi> 194</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">The orig. probably read <hi rend="italic">healf penig</hi>, according to <hi rend="italic">Wb obolata</hi>. <hi rend="italic">Cf. Gl</hi> Halbpfennig; on market toll Maitland <hi rend="italic">Domesday</hi> 194</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IVAtr_2.lnote.de.3" n="3" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">163</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Nicht alle Schiffe (<hi rend="italic">s. d. Gl</hi> 1) waren damals Einmaster; <hi rend="italic">vgl</hi>. Nicolas <hi rend="italic">Hist. Engl. navy</hi> I 22; <hi rend="italic">se mæsta, medemesta, lesta segl</hi> bei Toller 856b</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Not all ships (<hi rend="italic">see</hi> that word in <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi> 1) were single-masted at that time; <hi rend="italic">cf</hi>. Nicolas <hi rend="italic">Hist. Engl. navy</hi> I 22; <hi rend="italic">se mæsta, medemesta, lesta segl</hi> in Toller 856b</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="IVAtr_2.robnote.n.1" n="1" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">322</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">This is the earliest mention of this important quay under this name. Lethaby (<hi rend="italic">op. cit.</hi> p. 92) suggests that it was probably the <hi rend="italic">Lundentuneshythe</hi> mentioned in a charter of 743–5 (see Earle, <hi rend="italic">Land Charters</hi>, p. 42). Toll was paid there under Richard I (see Madox, <hi rend="italic">Hist. of the Exchequer</hi>, <hi rend="smallcaps">i</hi>. pp. 775, 778, 779) and under Edward III (see Stow, <hi rend="italic">op. cit.</hi> p. 206). Stow describes it as ‘at this present (<hi rend="italic">i.e.</hi> 1598) a large watergate, port or harbour for ships and boats commonly arriving there with fish both fresh and salt, oranges, onions and other fruits and roots, wheat, rye and grain of divers sorts.’ Its importance as a fish market dates from 1699 (see 10 &amp; 11 William III. c. 24).</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="IVAtr_2.robnote.fn.12" n="12" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">70</biblScope></bibl><label>tolonei</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">thel.</hi> T, Br.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="IVAtr_2.robnote.fn.13" n="13" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">70</biblScope></bibl><label>haberet</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">habet</hi> T, M, Hk, Br.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="IVAtr_2.robnote.fn.14" n="14" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">70</biblScope></bibl><label>siglas</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">gulas</hi> R, T.</seg></note>
      </div>
